  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The active pattern of recent weeks \nfinally comes to an end today as high pressure returns to \ndominance over the Great Basin. There is still a small chance for \nshowers or thunderstorms along the Utah border today, at about 5% \nto 10%. Starting tomorrow the high pressure system previously \nover the Great Plains will shift far enough west to entirely cut \noff moisture to the forecast area. The resulting drying trend will\nincrease fire weather concerns, especially when paired with \nbreezy afternoon winds 20-25 mph at times. The other main concern \nstarting tomorrow will be heat. High temperatures will be five to \nten degrees above normal and climb into the triple digits by \nFriday. Overnight temperatures will also be around ten degrees \nabove normal and the combination of the two indicates a moderate \nrisk for heat related illnesses, creeping into major risk for \nFriday and the weekend.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Low confidence in a few final\nthunderstorms today. High confidence in dry and warming conditions\nand moderate confidence in intense heat impacts by late week. No\nchanges were made to NBM output.",
